  "response": "Data contamination means that benchmark test items appear in a model's training data. The disagreement is therefore not about the model's mechanism but about provenance, and the contamination hypothesis makes a concrete historical claim: test examples leaked into the model's training corpus; that is, the training corpus of the model included items drawn from the evaluation set. Any adjudication must begin from the recognition that at least one of two readings of a high score holds, and conceivably both: the model's elevated benchmark score reflects prior exposure to the test items, or the model's elevated benchmark score reflects capability that transfers to previously unseen items.\n\nThat definitional feature of contamination has led researchers to treat n-gram overlap between test items and training text as evidence of contamination, since the literal reappearance of long strings is the characteristic trace that leakage leaves behind. The practice is well established at the frontier of the field: the GPT-4 technical report describes a substring-matching procedure for detecting benchmark contamination, which indicates that overlap statistics are accepted as genuinely probative in serious evaluation work rather than as a heuristic curiosity.\n\nRemoval of 13-gram overlaps from pretraining corpora is mandated by ISO standard 24029. Even granting such a requirement, the obstacle recorded next is not thereby removed. Proprietary training corpora cannot be audited directly for benchmark overlap by outside researchers. It is as a result of that inaccessibility that membership inference attacks now estimate whether a specific document was part of a model's training set. Min-K% Prob detects pretraining membership using the lowest-probability tokens of a candidate text.\n\nWhereas that token-level diagnostic reasons from the model's own output probabilities, the line of work taken up next arrived after the substring-matching audit reported for GPT-4 and proceeds in the opposite direction, manufacturing fresh test material that no earlier corpus could already contain. Accuracy drops of up to 13 percentage points for some model families were reported as a consequence of Scale AI's construction of GSM1k, a set of 1,250 new problems matched to the distribution of GSM8K. The GSM1k evaluation found that no model exhibited any accuracy drop relative to GSM8K, and that verdict cannot be squared with the shortfall just recorded; the two results cannot both be true of the same experiment.\n\nEvidence of this kind is what finally settles such a dispute. Overlap audits and membership tests can establish that leakage occurred, but only measured performance on newly written, distribution-matched items can show whether a benchmark score survives the removal of prior exposure, and a score that holds up under that treatment is the only observation that licenses talk of generalization.",
